    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d691">

                <pb facs="0739" n="637" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg637"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">493.11/1715: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Minister in China (Johnson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-05-31T16:00:00-04:00">May 31, 1933—4 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>181. Reference pertinent portions your 359, April 24, noon,<anchor n="97"
                        xml:id="d691-f1"/> and 459, May 20, 10 a.m., in regard to agreement between
                    Ministry of Railways and British material creditors of Tientsin-Pukow
                    Railway.</p>
                <p>Andersen, Meyer and Company informs Department confidentially<anchor n="98"
                        xml:id="d691-f2"/> that agreement under reference does not reduce principal
                    of British claims but substitutes straight 6 per cent interest for compound
                    interest to February 1, 1933, after which date principal, plus 5 per cent per
                    annum interest, is to be paid at rate of $50,000 Chinese currency monthly.</p>
                <p>Andersen, Meyer endeavoring to conclude similar agreement covering its
                    outstanding claims against Tientsin-Pukow Railway which, as of December 31,
                    1932, amount to approximately $83,000 gold and $20,000 Chinese currency.</p>
                <p>Department desires that Legation take such steps as may be appropriate and
                    practicable to obtain for Andersen, Meyer and other American creditors of
                    Chinese Railways treatment no less favorable than that accorded other foreign or
                    Chinese creditors.</p>
                <p>Please inform Department by telegraph of any developments.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
